{
  // Incremental adoption, not a repo-wide requirement: checkJs is false here
  // deliberately, so no .js file is checked unless it opts in with its own
  // "// @ts-check" comment at the top (works regardless of this checkJs
  // setting - that's the whole mechanism). Opt a file in only once its
  // JSDoc @param/@returns tags are specific enough that tsc won't just flag
  // "property X does not exist on type object" everywhere - a too-generic
  // {object} type is worse than no type, since it hides real shape info.
  // Run via `npm run typecheck`.
  //
  // As of 2026-07-31, every file under js/ has opted in and passes clean -
  // full current coverage. Deliberately NOT flipping checkJs to true to
  // "lock that in": this repo's dependencies include files outside js/ (e.g.
  // supabase-public.js, imported by several js/*.js modules) that are not
  // opted in and do not yet typecheck cleanly. With checkJs:false + per-file
  // opt-in, importing an unchecked file is silently fine (only the imported
  // file's own JSDoc-declared exports are checked at the call site, never
  // its internals) - checkJs:true would instead force-check every such
  // dependency's full body too, breaking CI on files nobody has opted in
  // yet. A new js/ file still starts unchecked until it adds its own
  // "// @ts-check" - the same deliberate gate as when this file had only 3.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "checkJs": false,
    "allowJs": true,
    "strict": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["js/**/*.js", "js/global.d.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "js/vendor/**"]
}
